  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper and appendices are present. Some MLLMU tables are flattened with fewer numeric entries than method labels, so those values cannot be assigned reliably; they are not used in rows.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates unlearning and retention with behavioral measures, including GA and other methods, but does not test the recovery attacks in the claim.",
    "gate_note": "The CLEAR results in Table 2 directly report forgetting, retention, response coherence, and real-question accuracy after unlearning. The paper does not apply recovery fine-tuning, activation-direction ablation, quantization, or prompt-only attacks. MLLMU Table 1 and some ablation tables have ambiguous flattened method/value alignment and are not assigned to rows. Token-distribution visualizations and the theoretical gradient analysis do not test recovery or establish that knowledge is deleted versus access-blocked.",
    "test_reason": "A new-method paper in a new setting (multimodal LLM, LLaVA-7B, MLLMU and CLEAR). It reports forget-set accuracy, retention and coherence after GA, NPO, IdkPO and ViKeR, which bears on the first half of version A. It applies no recovery attack (relearning, ablation, quantization or prompting), so B–F are untested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that ViKeR achieves unlearning comparable to baselines while better preserving retention and response coherence on MLLMU and CLEAR. They make no claim about robustness to recovery attacks or about whether knowledge is deleted.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "ViKeR adds KL regularization toward token distributions averaged over irrelevant visual inputs to the GA loss; it is not a recovery-attack recipe.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports outcome variation with regularization strength λ, number of irrelevant reference images k, and reference-image category. It reports that larger λ improves retention but weakens unlearning; small k can destabilize results; reference-image choice affects unlearning and retention.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Token-distribution visualizations (Fig 4, Fig 11, Fig 13, App C.2) show NPO and IdkPO keep high probability on key private tokens when the ground-truth prefix is supplied. The authors say they 'may still produce the correct answers when provided with the corresponding prefix'. IdkPO evades answering rather than forgetting. These are single examples with no rates.",
        "proposed_claim": "Prefix or prefill conditioning exposes residual target knowledge after refusal-style or NPO unlearning in MLLMs (closest to E, or C's access-blocking).",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The study evaluates one stated MLLM backbone (LLaVA-7B); no recovery attacks are tested. CLEAR chance level and unmodified reference values for the reported metrics are not stated in Table 2. Some MLLMU results are not assignable because the flattened tables show mismatched method labels and numeric entries.",
    "rows_left_out": "MLLMU Table 1 and flattened ablation tables were not extracted because the visible method labels and value counts do not support reliable assignment. Token-distribution figures and theoretical gradient results are not recovery tests.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "A",
        "parts": "Whether simple attacks recover the forgotten knowledge. No attack is applied to any method."
      },
      {
        "version": "B",
        "parts": "All: no relearning fine-tuning."
      },
      {
        "version": "C",
        "parts": "All: no activation ablation or deletion-versus-blocking analysis beyond single-example token plots."
      },
      {
        "version": "D",
        "parts": "All: no quantization."
      },
      {
        "version": "E",
        "parts": "No rephrasing, jailbreak, language or in-context attack. There is only a qualitative prefix observation (see fits_no_version)."
      },
      {
        "version": "F",
        "parts": "ViKeR is a new method but is not tested against any recovery attack."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "MLLMU results. Forget ACC (reading the flattened table as Origin, GA, NPO, IdkPO, ViKeR): 10% task 25.2 reference vs 25.3/30.8/36.9/30.4. Retain ROUGE falls from Full 49.7 to 20.6/29.7/5.7/32.4. At 15%, GA gives 0.0 everywhere, including GIB. Retention drops substantially for every method.",
        "locator": "Table 1; Tables 3–4",
        "versions": "A"
      },
      {
        "what": "The ViKeR λ sweep shows a trade-off between forgetting and retention. At 15%, λ=0.1 collapses (GIB 0.0), and forget ACC rises from 29.8 to 42.0 as λ grows.",
        "locator": "Table 6",
        "versions": "A"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "Method paper, single backbone (LLaVA-7B, LoRA). CLEAR Table 2 gives no full-model reference for REC or Real QA ACC, so retention near zero is judged from the absolute floor and the paper's REC definition. The MLLMU table alignment is inferred because the extraction is flattened. The study cites SAM relearning-resilience and MLLM unlearning-attack work but runs no attacks."
  },
